    {
     "t": "Textbook: Marketing: Creating Customer Value and Engagement",
     "src": "Kotler & Armstrong 17e ch1",
     "h": "<p><strong>Needs, wants and demands</strong> — Needs are states of felt deprivation — physical, social and individual — that marketers do not create. Wants are the form a need takes once culture and personality shape it. Demands are wants backed by buying power; people then choose the bundle of benefits that gives the most value and satisfaction for their resources.<br><em>e.g.</em> Hunger is the need; masala dosa in Chennai or luchi in Kolkata is the want; the dish you can afford with the money in your pocket is the demand.</p><p><strong>Market offerings and marketing myopia</strong> — A market offering is any mix of products, services, information or experiences offered to satisfy a need or want; it can also be a person, place, organisation or idea. Marketing myopia is the error of falling in love with your own product and forgetting the underlying need it serves, which leaves the firm exposed when a better solution arrives.<br><em>e.g.</em> A drill-bit maker that thinks customers want drill bits, when they really want holes, is myopic.</p>"
    }
